WEBVTT

00:00.530 --> 00:01.710
- [Instructor] Let's say we wanna solve

00:01.710 --> 00:03.340
the following equation for x.

00:03.340 --> 00:08.340
We have x plus one over nine
minus x is equal to 2/3.

00:09.440 --> 00:11.540
Pause this video and
see if you can try this

00:11.540 --> 00:13.145
before we work through it together.

00:13.145 --> 00:16.390
All right now let's work
through this together.

00:16.390 --> 00:18.530
Now, the first thing
that we might wanna do,

00:18.530 --> 00:20.150
there's several ways that
you could approach this,

00:20.150 --> 00:22.140
but the thing I like to do is

00:22.140 --> 00:24.510
get rid of this x here in the denominator.

00:24.510 --> 00:26.681
And the easiest way I
can think of doing that,

00:26.681 --> 00:29.090
is by multiplying both sides

00:29.090 --> 00:34.050
of this equation by nine minus x.

00:34.050 --> 00:35.550
Now, when you do that,

00:35.550 --> 00:38.100
it's important that you
then put the qualifier

00:38.100 --> 00:40.530
that the x cannot be equal to the value

00:40.530 --> 00:43.080
that would have made this
denominator zero 'cause clearly

00:43.080 --> 00:45.650
if somehow you do all this
algebraic manipulation

00:45.650 --> 00:47.500
and you got x is equal to nine

00:47.500 --> 00:49.190
that still wouldn't be a valid solution

00:49.190 --> 00:50.990
'cause if you were to substitute nine

00:50.990 --> 00:52.730
back into the original equation

00:52.730 --> 00:55.390
you'd be dividing by
zero in the denominator.

00:55.390 --> 00:57.050
So, let's just put that right over here,

00:57.050 --> 01:00.890
x cannot be equal to nine.

01:00.890 --> 01:03.760
And so then, we can safely move ahead

01:03.760 --> 01:05.690
with our algebraic manipulations.

01:05.690 --> 01:07.240
So on the left-hand side,

01:07.240 --> 01:10.910
as long as x does not
equal nine, if we multiply,

01:10.910 --> 01:13.480
and divided by nine
minus x they cancel out,

01:13.480 --> 01:15.950
and we'll just be left with an x plus one,

01:15.950 --> 01:17.360
and on the right-hand side,

01:17.360 --> 01:20.080
if you multiply 2/3 times nine minus x,

01:20.080 --> 01:23.240
we get 2/3 times nine is six

01:23.240 --> 01:27.447
and then 2/3 times negative
x is negative 2/3 x

01:28.460 --> 01:30.490
and once again, let's remind ourselves,

01:30.490 --> 01:33.220
that x cannot be equal to nine.

01:33.220 --> 01:36.180
And then we can get all of
our x's on the same side

01:36.180 --> 01:37.390
so let's out that on the left.

01:37.390 --> 01:39.840
So let's add 2/3 x to both sides.

01:39.840 --> 01:44.840
So plus 2/3, 2/3 x plus 2/3 x,

01:46.600 --> 01:49.440
and then, what do we have?

01:49.440 --> 01:52.320
Well, on the left-hand side we have one x

01:52.320 --> 01:56.670
which is the same thing
as 3/3 x plus 2/3 x

01:56.670 --> 02:01.670
is going to give us 5/3 x
plus one is equal to six,

02:03.950 --> 02:08.120
and then these characters cancel out.

02:08.120 --> 02:11.490
And then we can just
subtract one from both sides,

02:11.490 --> 02:16.490
and we get 5/3 x, 5/3 x is equal to five.

02:18.810 --> 02:20.290
And then, last but not least,

02:20.290 --> 02:22.310
we can multiply both
sides of this equation,

02:22.310 --> 02:24.410
times the reciprocal of 5/3

02:24.410 --> 02:26.220
which is of course 3/5,

02:26.220 --> 02:27.130
and I'm doing that

02:27.130 --> 02:30.120
so I just have an x isolated
on the left-hand side.

02:30.120 --> 02:31.700
So times 3/5,

02:31.700 --> 02:34.660
and we are left with 3/5 times 5/3

02:34.660 --> 02:36.560
is of course equal to one.

02:36.560 --> 02:41.560
So we're left with x is equal
to five times 3/5 is three.

02:42.080 --> 02:44.040
And so we're feeling pretty
good about x equals three,

02:44.040 --> 02:46.740
but we have to make sure
that that's consistent

02:46.740 --> 02:48.780
with our original expression.

02:48.780 --> 02:49.870
Well if we look up here,

02:49.870 --> 02:52.220
or if you substitute back x equals three,

02:52.220 --> 02:53.950
you don't get a zero in the denominator,

02:53.950 --> 02:55.700
x is not equal to nine.

02:55.700 --> 02:57.720
X equals three is consistent with that.

02:57.720 --> 02:59.870
So we should feel good about our solution.

02:59.870 --> 03:01.430
If we did all this algebraic manipulation

03:01.430 --> 03:03.250
and we get an x is equal to nine,

03:03.250 --> 03:05.070
then that still wouldn't
be a valid solution

03:05.070 --> 03:06.770
because it would have made

03:06.770 --> 03:10.223
the original expression
on the left be undefined.
